# Intent: make a real rub register without bringing the phantom pets back (owner report 2026-10-21: after 048 "I don't see rubbing registering"). The host log showed a rub reads only pad value 1 and never 2, and the lockout for three short contacts also fired on his rubbing. Contact is now told apart by how long and how dense it is, not by strength. A press is 6 touched samples out of the last 8 (reading 1 or more), with the contact at least 8 samples (400 ms) old and the current sample touched, so a rub with dropouts counts and an isolated blip or a short tap does not. It ends when at most 1 of the last 8 samples is touched. A blip is now a run of 1 or 2 touched samples with 8 quiet samples before and after it; three blips inside 10 s still lock the strip out for 10 s, but a rub's dropouts no longer count as blips. head_touch_stats gains run_max (the longest unbroken touched run in samples) and long_runs (runs of 8 samples or more), so the host log shows what a rub looks like.
# Upstream: m5stack/StackChan firmware/main/hal/hal_head_touch.cpp (MIT). Applies after 051 with --unidiff-zero.
# Check after a flash: leave it alone for 5 minutes: head_touch_stats shows presses=0 and long_runs=0. Then rub the head for a few seconds: presses=1 or more, long_runs=1 or more, and a pet is reported to the host. A simulation of the recogniser (steady rub, a rub with dropouts, isolated blips every 10 s, short taps, a 1 s hold) gave the expected results before this patch was written.
